@node Introduction
@section Introduction

The builtin functions are not immediately accessible after startup.
At startup they must first be linked to a macro name using the @code{0_define} macro. This is the only macro available at startup.

The @var{arguments} should be placed on the first stack (stack a) by using a pattern. The pattern @code{0} is defined at startup. The syntax of this pattern is used in the examples below.
The first argument is placed on position 1 on the stack. The position 0 on the stack has the name of the macro.

In the description below the arguments with square hooks ([...]) indicate optional arguments. The arguments are not placed in a defined syntax, because you can determine the syntax!

@node Functions for defining macros
@section Functions for defining macros

This is probably the most complex macro to start with, but since it is the first macro to be used it is best to understand first.


@deffn builtin define name [definition] {[builtin function]} {macro-settings} {[argument-pattern]} {[substitute-pattern]} {[program]} {[macro-set]}

This function defines a new macro. The @code{0_define} macro can be used for this at startup. For an overview how the options for the different parts function @pxref{The complete working of macros}.

The @var{name} is the name of the new macro, must exist and have at least one character. The @var{name} can consist of any character or byte or a set of characters or bytes. Characters can be grouped with straight hooks (left hook [ and right hook ] are the default) to form a set at the specific position in the name.
For example:
@example
[Nn]ame
@end example
means the word "name" starting with a small or capital n.
Also special characters defined with @code{specialc} (@pxref{Functions for setting symbols}) can be used to define sets or strings.

The @var{name} can have a maximum of 64 positions. This is a limit invoked by the implementation and can not be overcome without substantial changes to the program. 

The @ovar{definition} replaces the macro name.
The @ovar{definition} is normally used in user macros and normally not when linking to builtin macros. However it can be used with builtin macros to output a text different from the output of the builtin.
Special codes or words (e.g. @code{$1} as in @code{m4}) can be used to fill in the arguments of the macro. 

The @ovar{builtin function} determines the builtin function to be called. This function will be called irrespective of the other options and settings. The pattern for collecting arguments and / or the program should have placed all the necessary arguments on the stack for the correct execution of the builtin function.  


The @var{macro-settings} should be set, otherwise the default (@code{nn00}) will be used. This default is normally not very useful.
The @var{macro-settings} is a string of a specific length with characters at a specific position having a specific meaning.
The positions in the string have the meanings: 
@table @code
@item 1
Is the definition recursively processed (@code{r}) or not (@code{n} or anything other than @code{r}) by the macro processor.
@item 2
Is recursion for macro recognition during processing of the arguments active (@code{r}) or not (@code{n} or anything other than @code{r}).  
@item 3
Number (hexadecimal; 0 -- f) defining the pre-size of the macro name.
@item 4
Number (hexadecimal) defining the post-size of the macro name.
@item 5
Optional virtual character. This character is output to the algorithm but not to the output. This can be used if a pre-size is set in macros so that these macros can be recognised after a preceding macro. Mostly for emulation of macros as words surrounded by white space.
An example of the difference (see the difference at the @code{"rr00"}):

@example
# A simple define.
0_define:Define:;;define;nr01;0;;stack_d "" putarg3 "rr00"
putarg4 "0" putarg5 "" putarg6 "" putarg7 

Define:test;Hello World!
Define:aa;te
Define:bb;st

aabb

# A simple define with virtual char.
0_define:Define_:;;define;nr01;0;;stack_d "" putarg3 "rr00 "
putarg4 "0" putarg5 "" putarg6 "" putarg7 

Define_:cc;te
Define_:dd;st

ccdd
@end example

This example will result in:

@example
# A simple define.


Hello World!

# A simple define with virtual char.


test
 
@end example
@end table

An example of @var{macro-settings}:
@example
nr01 
@end example
The meaning of this is: no macro recursion, recursion during argument processing, no (0) pre-size, post-size of 1.

The @ovar{argument-pattern} is the name of the pattern to be used for collecting arguments.

The @ovar{substitute-pattern} is the name of the pattern to be used for substitution of special characters with the arguments in the definition. If this option is not used the default argument substitution is used. If this option exists but the definition string is empty, then the first argument is used in place of the definition. This can be used for macros such as the @code{format} macro of @code{m4}.

The @ovar{program} is a program that is executed just after the arguments are collected and before the builtin macro is executed or argument substitution. This can be used to set arguments to fixed values.

The optional @ovar{macro-set} is the name of a set of macros that will be used to define the macro in. If this option is not set, the macro is defined in the current active set of macros. The default macro set name at start is @code{0}.
If the macro set does not yet exist, it will be initialised.


The only macro @code{0_define} available at the start using the pattern @code{0} for argument collection is defined as:
@example
0_define:0_define:;;define;nr01;0

@end example
This definition has
@table @code
@item 0_define:
(second @code{0_define:}) as the name of the macro. It is including the start (@code{:}) of the argument collection.
@item define
Is the builtin function.  
@item nr01
The @var{macro-settings} meaning: no macro recursion (not necessary since no output), recursion during argument processing, no (0) pre-size, post-size of 1 (this is the @code{:} of the argument collection).
@item 0
The pattern (named @code{0}) for argument collection.
@end table

Output: none.
@end deffn



@deffn builtin push name [definition] {[builtin function]} {macro-settings} {[argument-pattern]} {[substitute-pattern]} {[program]} {[macro-set]}

Similar to define, but saves the previous definition. The previous definition can be retrieved with @code{pop}.
It redefines the user macro @var{name} using the same arguments as used in @code{define}.

Output: none.
@end deffn



@deffn builtin undefine name [macro-set]
Deletes the macro @var{name}. The macro does not exist anymore after this.

If the @ovar{macro-set} is used then the macro is deleted from this macro set.
If @ovar{macro-set} is not defined, then the current macro set is used.
If the macro set named in @ovar{macro-set} does not exist, an error is output.

Output: none.
@end deffn



@deffn builtin pop name [macro-set]
Retrieves the previous definition of the macro @var{name}.

If the @ovar{macro-set} is used then the macro is retrieved using this macro set.
If @ovar{macro-set} is not defined, then the current macro set is used.
If the macro set named in @ovar{macro-set} does not exist, an error is output.

Output: none.
@end deffn



@node Macro sets
@section Macro sets

Macros are defined in a set of macros. This allows to have macros active at different times when processing 
an input and not having to spilt an input and processing the parts separately. 

Only one set of macros can be active at one time. It is thus not so flexible as to be able to activate and deactivate certain macros.

The number of macro sets has no impact on normal performance. There is however more memory used to store the macro sets.

@deffn builtin macroset macro-set
Switches to the macro set with name @var{macro-set}. This macro set must already exist, otherwise this is an error and the macro set is not changed.

Only macros available in the new macro set can be used after switching. The function @code{define} can for example be used to define macros in a macro set.

Output: none.
@end deffn

@deffn builtin defmcall name [called-macro] {[called macro-set]} {mcall-settings} {argument-pattern} {[program]} {[macro-set]} 

Defines a macro that will call a macro (@code{mcall}) in a macro set. The @code{mcall} enables the call to a macro in another macro set
without first switching to this macro set.

The @code{mcall} is similar to a macro, except that it calls another macro instead of a built in function or a definition.
It calls the macro with the name in @ovar{called-macro} or when this is empty the macro name in first argument on the stack when the @code{mcall} is invoked.
It will collect the arguments using the @var{argument-pattern} and run the @ovar{program} before the
called macro is executed.

The called macro will not perform argument collection since this has already been done by the @code{mcall}.
This has the implication that the syntax for argument collection as defined in the @code{mcall}
(by the @var{argument-pattern}) is used and not the syntax as defined in the called macro.

Apart from the argument collection, the called macro will execute all the defined settings in the macro set
of the called macro (the @ovar{called macro-set}).
This means that if macros are used in the definition string of the called macro, these 
macros should exist in the called macro set if they should expand there and not
expand in the current macro set.

The @var{name} is the name of the new macro call, must exist and has the same possibilities as a macro name.

The @ovar{called-macro} is the name of the called macro. The definition of the called macro at the time of the @code{mcall} is used.
If the @ovar{called-macro} is not defined, the first argument on the stack is used as the called macro name.
If the called macro does not exist an error will result.

If the first argument on the stack is used as the called macro name, the stack is adapted so that the called macro
will see the arguments shifted by one. The first argument for the called macro is the second argument for the @code{mcall}.
For the called macro, its name is on position zero of the stack.

The called macro is expanded in the @ovar{called macro-set}. If the @ovar{called macro-set} is not defined the current macro set
is used as the macro set for the called macro. If the called macro set does not exist, it will be initialised.

The @var{mcall-settings} is similar to the @var{macro-settings} in @code{define} and should be set. Otherwise the default (@code{nn00}) will be used. This default is normally not very useful.
The @var{mcall-settings} is a string of a specific length with characters at a specific position having a specific meaning.
The positions in the string have the meanings: 
@table @code
@item 1
Is the output of the called macro recursively processed (@code{r}) or not (@code{n} or anything other than @code{r}) by the macro processor.
The recursive processing can be overridden by the called macro using the instruction @code{recur_y} or @code{recur_n}.
@item 2
Is recursion for macro recognition during processing of the arguments active (@code{r}) or not (@code{n} or anything other than @code{r}).  
@item 3
Number (hexadecimal; 0 -- f) defining the pre-size of the macro name.
@item 4
Number (hexadecimal) defining the post-size of the macro name.
@item 5
Optional virtual character. This character is output to the algorithm but not to the output. This can be used if a pre-size is set in macros so that these macros can be recognised after a preceding macro. Mostly for emulation of macros as words surrounded by white space.
@end table

The @ovar{argument-pattern} is the name of the pattern to be used for collecting arguments in the same way as for macros.

The @ovar{program} is a program that is executed just after the arguments are collected in the same way as for macros.

The optional @ovar{macro-set} is the name of a set of macros that will be used to define the macro call in. If this option is not set, the macro call is defined in the current active set of macros.
If the macro set does not yet exist, it will be initialised.

The @code{undefine} function can be used to delete the @code{mcall}.

Output: none.



The following figure is a symbolic overview of the working of @code{mcall} when the
@ovar{called-macro} holds the name of a macro:

@example
@group
@verbatim
in:  | | | |m|a|c|r|o| |n|a|m|e|(|a|r|g|u|m|e|n|t|s|)| | |
           +-------------------+-+-------------------+
                macro name     : :     arguments
           +-+                 +-+            
         pre-size            post-size     |
                               : :         v
                               : :  
                               | | | | | | | | | | | | |
                               +-+---+-----+-+----------
                           pattern for collecting arguments +
                                     small programs
    
                                           |
                                           |          argument n
                                           +----->    ----------
                                                         ...
       optional program for stack manipulation -->    ----------
                                                      argument 3
                                                      ----------
                                                      argument 2
   [name of macro] --->  called macro  <---           ----------  
                                                      argument 1  
                             |                        ----------
                             v    
          
out: | | | | |m|a|c|r|o| |o|u|t|p|u|t| | | | | | | | | | |
