@deffn builtin pshmcall name [called-macro] {[called macro-set]} {mcall-settings} {argument-pattern} {[program]} {[macro-set]} 
Push macro call is similar to @code{defmcall}, but saves the previous definition. The previous definition can be retrieved with @code{pop}.
It redefines the macro call @var{name} using the same arguments as used in @code{defmcall}.

Output: none.
@end deffn




@node Functions for defining patterns
@section Functions for defining patterns

@deffn builtin pattern name pattern-part program [priority]
This functions adds @var{pattern-part} to a pattern named @var{name}.
A pattern with @var{name} is automatically initialised when not already used before. 

See @ref{Patterns} for the possibilities of the @var{pattern-part}.

The program to be executed when the pattern part is triggered is defined in @var{program}.
Normally every pattern part has a program, but it can be left out.

The @ovar{priority} is used in a special case.
Normally a pattern will not be triggered if at the same position a macro is triggered.
This is based on the logic that a macro will change the text and that at this position
no pattern should also be triggered with the old input.
The @ovar{priority} can overrule this behaviour by setting a priority of 1 or larger.

An example of a special case where this is used is the pattern for argument collection 
in the @code{m4} emulation:
@example
@verbatim
0_pattern:m4;[(,][@00- ]*[!-@ff];stack_a begin-1 ;1
@end verbatim
@end example

This pattern part sets the begin of an argument after optional whitespace 
(@code{[@@00- ]*}) and 
has to include a first non whitespace character (@code{[!-@@ff]}). It will set the 
begin of the argument directly before the non whitespace character (the @code{begin-1} instruction).
The non whitespace can however also be a macro, like the quote macro.
In this case both the pattern part and the macro should be triggered.

Output: none.
@end deffn


@deffn builtin append_p name pattern-part program [priority]
This function appends @var{pattern-part} to the previous pattern part in the pattern named @var{name}.
It has the same arguments as @code{pattern}.

The pattern-part matching will only start when the previously defined pattern-part has triggered.

Output: none.
@end deffn


@deffn builtin clr_pat name
Clears an existing pattern named @var{name}.

The pattern still exists after this function call, but it is empty.
This can be used to redefine a syntax.

Output: none.
@end deffn

@deffn builtin copy_pat from to
Copies an existing pattern named @var{from} to @var{to}.

This can be used to reuse a pattern and extend it further in a new pattern.

Output: none.
@end deffn


@node Functions for setting symbols
@section Functions for setting symbols


@deffn builtin specialc {special-character} string
This function defines a special character (@var{special-character}) that expands to @var{string}
in the name of macros or in pattern-parts.

This can be used as a short means of writing repeatedly longer strings. The special character
is used by preceding it with "@code{\}" in macro names or pattern parts. This preceding character can be set using the @code{charpat} function.

The @var{special-character} is a single character and can have any byte value.

A redefinition of the special character has no influence on already use of it in macro names and pattern parts.

Output: none.
@end deffn

@deffn builtin charpat pattern-settings
This function defines the characters with a special meaning used in macro names and pattern parts.

The @var{pattern-settings} is a string of a specific length (9 characters) with characters at a specific position having a specific meaning.
The positions in the string have the meanings: 

@table @code
@item 1
Is the character used as opening brace for character sets. This is "@code{[}" by default.
@item 2
Is the character used as closing brace for character sets. This is "@code{]}" by default.
@item 3
Is the character used to indicate range in a character set. This is "@code{-}" by default.
@item 4
Is the character used to indicate a special character. This is "@code{\}" by default.
@item 5
Is the character used to input a character by hexadecimal ASCII code. This is "@code{@@}" by default.
@item 6
Is the character used for the one or more characters in a character set. This is "@code{+}" by default.
@item 7
Is the character used for the zero or more characters in a character set. This is "@code{*}" by default.
@item 8
Is the character used for the zero or one character in a character set. This is "@code{?}" by default.
@item 9
Is the character used for a one time trigger of a character set. This is "@code{~}" by default.
@end table

The default @var{pattern-settings} is thus: @code{[]-\@@+*?~}

Output: none.
@end deffn


@node chararg
@deffn builtin chararg {argument-settings} {variable-quote-start} {variable-quote-end} {variable-separator} {[macro-set]}

This function is used to define the characters for the argument substitution when using the the default
argument substitution. Only a single character can be selected. This is similar to @code{m4}.

The @var{argument-settings} is a string of a specific length (4 or 5 characters) with characters at a specific position having a specific meaning.
The positions in the string have the meanings: 

@table @code
@item 1
Is the character used for arguments in the definition. This is @code{$} by default. The number after this character in the definition is the entry of the stack.
@item 2
Is the character used to output all arguments. This character is put after the first character in a definition. This is @code{*} by default.
@item 3
Is the character used to output all arguments with quotes. This character is put after the first character in a definition. This is @code{@@} by default.
@item 4
Is the character used to output the number of arguments. This character is put after the first character in a definition. This is @code{#} by default.
@item 5
Is the optional character used for arguments from the other stacks in the definition. This is @code{$} by default. 
An @code{a} -- @code{f} after this character in the definition selects the stack. The number after this is the entry of the stack. 
If this is not set, the arguments from the other stacks can not be selected. 
@end table

The @var{variable-quote-start}, @var{variable-quote-end} and @var{variable-separator} are the numbers of the variables where the characters or strings of the start of a quote,
the end of a quote and the separator of arguments are stored. See also the function @code{set_var} in @ref{Functions for using variables and system information} for
defining these characters or strings. 


The optional @ovar{macro-set} is the name of a set of macros that will be used to set the argument characters for. If this option is not set, the argument characters are set in the current active set of macros. The default macro set name at start is @code{0}.
If the macro set does not yet exist, it will be initialised.

The default is defined as:

@example
@verbatim
0_define:0_chars_args:;;chararg;nr01;0

0_chars_args:$*@#$;1;2;3
@end verbatim
@end example

Output: none.

@end deffn



@node String functions
@section String functions

@deffn builtin strindex string substring
This function returns the position of @var{substring} in the @var{string}.

The first position has number 0.

If the @var{substring} is not found the number -1 is returned.

Output: position of the @var{substring} in @var{string} or -1.

@end deffn

@deffn builtin substr string from [length]
Expands to the substring of @var{string}, which starts at @var{from}, and extends for @ovar{length}
characters, or to the end of @var{string}, if @ovar{length} is omitted. The starting index of a string
is 0. 

Output: the substring
@end deffn

@deffn builtin strtrans string chars [replacement]
Translates the characters in @var{chars} in the @var{string} to corresponding characters in @ovar{replacement}.
Corresponding characters have the same position in @var{chars} and @ovar{replacement}.

If a character in @var{chars} has no corresponding character in @ovar{replacement} it is deleted.

Also a range of characters can be given, e.g.: @code{a-z} or @code{z-a}.

Output: translated string
@end deffn

@deffn builtin num2chr number
This function returns the character with the ASCII @var{number}.
The number is therefore at least 0 and maximum 255. If the number is
outside of this range nothing is output.

Output: single character
@end deffn

@deffn builtin num2str number [radix] [width]
Converts a @var{number} to a string.

By default the @var{number} is output as decimal. It can also be output in another base
by setting @ovar{radix}. The @ovar{radix} can be 2 -- 36. The radix prefix is not in 
the output.

The minimum width is set by @ovar{width}. The number is padded with zeros.

A custom output can be made by using a definition string and using outputs placed in arguments.
The arguments that can be used for this are:
@table @code
@item 4
The radix prefix.
@item 5
Positive binary output. The @var{number} is treated as unsigned.
@item 6
A minus sign if the @var{number} is negative.
@item 7
The leading zeros.
@item 8
String of the @var{number} as in the output without leading zeros or minus sign. 
@end table


Output: string of @var{number}
@end deffn




@node Functions for using variables and system information
@section Functions for using variables and system information

A set of variables to store strings is available. 
They can be used to store information without using macros.
They are also used to give information to the standard arguments fill in function
for the characters or strings to use in the output of multiple arguments.

These variables are referenced by a number from 1 to 255.
Negative numbers are used to certain get system or process information. 


@deffn builtin set_var number string
Sets the variable with @var{number} to the value of @var{string}.
The @var{number} can be from @code{1} to @code{255}.

Output: none.

@end deffn

@deffn builtin get_var number
Outputs a string from the variable with @var{number}.

The @var{number} can  be:
@table @code
@item 1 - 255
Gets the string from the variable with @var{number}.
@item -1
Gets the diversion number.
@item -2
Gets the string of the operating system type e.g. @code{unix}.
@item -3
Gets the current global line number. This counts all new lines starting from the first input file.
@item -4
Gets the current local line number. This counts all new lines in the current input file.
@item -5
Gets the current input file name.
@item -6
Gets the program name.
@item -7
Gets a string with all the options after the @code{--} on the command line.
@item -8
Gets the return value from the last command executed by the function @code{shell}.
@end table

Output: the selected string.

@end deffn




@node Functions for getting macro information
@section Functions for getting macro information

@deffn builtin ifmacro? name [yes] [no] {[macro-set]}

This function checks if the macro with @var{name} exists in the macro set @ovar{macro-set}.
If the macro exists the string in @ovar{yes} is output otherwise the string in @ovar{no} is output. 

If @ovar{macro-set} is not defined, then the current macro set is used.
If the macro set named in @ovar{macro-set} does not exist, an error is output.

Output: the string in @ovar{yes} or in @ovar{no}.

@end deffn

@deffn builtin info name

This function outputs the definition string of the macro with @var{name}.

The other settings of the macro are put in arguments. They can be used for a customized
output by using a definition.
The arguments that can be used for this are:
@table @code
@item 2
Definition string.
@item 3
The builtin function
@item 4
@var{macro-settings}
@item 5
Pattern for argument collection.
@item 6
Pattern for argument substitution.
@item 7
The optional program.
@item 8
Internal number of the macro.
@end table

Output: definition of @var{name}
@end deffn



@node Functions for input and output
@section Functions for input and output


Diversions are temporary memory buffers for storing the output.
The diversions can be retrieved at a later time or are automatically retrieved
at the end of the input files.

If a diversion is set, the output that would go to the normal output is stored in a buffer.
Thus if a diversion is set multiple times inside a macro, only the last setting
of the diversion has an influence on the output.

The memory buffers have a diversion number of one or larger. Diversion number 0 means
the normal output. At the end of the input files the remaining diversions are automatically output
in ascending order.

@deffn builtin divert [number]
Sets the diversion to @ovar{number}.
If the @ovar{number} is not given, it will be by default 0, the normal output.

A negative @ovar{number} will discard all output.

Output: none
@end deffn

@deffn builtin undivert [number] [number]
The @code{undivert} will empty the diversion with @ovar{number} into the output.
Multiple @ovar{number}s can be given to undivert.
If no number is given, all diversions are undiverted.

The contents of the diversion can still be recursed for macros if the @var{macro-settings} 
is properly set.

Output: diversion buffer
@end deffn

@deffn builtin include file
The @code{include} will read the @var{file} and output its contents.

The @var{macro-settings} of the definition will (like every macro) determine if the contents
of the @var{file} is recursed for macro recognition or not. Using a definition or definition fill pattern
does not seem useful.

If @var{file} does not exist an error is output and program execution is stopped.

Output: contents of @var{file}.
@end deffn

@deffn builtin sinclude file
Same as @code{include} but will fail silently if the @var{file} does not exist.

Output: contents of @var{file}.
@end deffn


@deffn builtin at_last string
Puts the @var{string} in a buffer that will be append to the input after the the end of the last file.
Similar to the @code{divert} function, but it will be automatically undiverted and used for macro recognition.

The function appends additional strings to the previous strings. It works thus like a FIFO buffer in the same
way as the @code{divert} function.

Output: none.
@end deffn

@deffn builtin shell command
Executes the @var{command} in a shell.
The @var{command} is executed in a shell with the stdin of @code{m0} as input and the stdout is redirected as input to @code{m0}.

Output: the output of stdout of the command.
@end deffn

@deffn builtin tempfile template
Creates a temporary file using a @var{template}.
The @var{template} should have six X (e.g. @code{tmp/docXXXXXX}) at the end for placement of a random string.
If these X are not in the @var{template}, the @var{template} will be expanded to hold the random string with six characters. 

The file is created and left empty. The function will output the name of the temporary file for further use. 

Output: name of the temporary file.
@end deffn




@node Functions for handling of errors
@section Functions for handling of errors

@deffn builtin errprint message [message]
This function will output @var{message} and following optionally multiple @ovar{message}
to standard error.

Output: none
@end deffn

@deffn builtin exit errorcode
This will exit the @code{m0} program directly with an @var{errorcode}.
The @var{errorcode} should be between 0 and 255.

Output: none
@end deffn




@node Some functions are missing?
@section Some functions are missing?

Several functions that exist in e.g. @code{m4} are not available in @code{m0}. Examples are:
@itemize
@item Conditionals like @code{if else}.
@item Arithmetic like @code{decr} to decrement a value or @code{eval} to evaluate a mathematical expression.
@item Quoting and comments
@item Shift macro
@item String functions like @code{len} for the length of a string or @code{format} to output formatted strings.
@end itemize

These functions are not available because they can be implemented using macros, patterns and programs.
Examples of these functions can be found in @ref{M4 emulation example}.